<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Wye</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Wye rises on Plynlimon in mid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and flows about 215 kilometres through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhayader">Rhayader</a>, Builth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> to join the Severn estuary near Chepstow. Its lower course forms part of the border between Wales and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it bounded kingdoms such as Gwent, Buellt and Morgannwg, and after 1067 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chepstow-castle">Chepstow Castle</a> above its mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-wye.jpg" alt="River Wye" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claire Ward · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Wye_at_Hay-on-Wye.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>2 kilometres from the source of the Severn. Although the source lies about 19 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>, the river at first runs inland. It passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhayader">Rhayader</a>, Builth Wells, Hay-on-Wye, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>, the only city on the river, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ross-on-wye">Ross-on-Wye</a>, Symonds Yat, Monmouth and Tintern, and meets the Severn Estuary just below Chepstow. The lower 26 kilometres, from Redbrook to Chepstow, form part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p><p>The Welsh name is Afon Gwy, and a mention of Guoy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a> of the early 9th century may be the earliest reference to the name, whose meaning is not clear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a bridge of wood and stone just upstream of present-day Chepstow. The river is tidal for about 24 kilometres up to Bigsweir, where hard rock forms a natural weir, and spring tides can raise the water by as much as 15 metres. Since Roman times it has carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> from the Forest of Dean, cider from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>, wine, iron, stone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>.</p><p>Early records are fragmentary, but iron appears to have been shipped down the river for the ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> in the 11th century. In 1171 and 1172 iron was supplied to Henry II for his invasion of Ireland. Under Edward I a common right of navigation was recorded, with the channel to be kept free of weirs.</p><p>Schemes to improve navigation followed. Sir William Sandys was appointed in 1662 to make the Wye and the Lugg navigable with flash locks, but the Wye ran too fast, the locks proved impracticable and costly, and the scheme was abandoned by 1668. The Rivers Wye and Lugg Navigation Act of 1695 authorised Herefordshire to buy and demolish the weirs and declared the rivers free and common. A towing path for horses from Lydbrook to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> was authorised in 1809, and barges reached Hereford in January 1811. Passenger boats ran from 1835. Trade declined after the Herefordshire and Gloucestershire Canal reached Hereford in 1845 and railways opened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lower valley is a National Landscape, and the river is protected by two Sites of Special Scientific Interest and designated a Special Area of Conservation. It was once known for its large spring salmon, the largest recorded weighing 59 lb 8 oz, caught in 1923. The river is used for canoeing, rowing and walking, and the Wye Valley Walk follows it from near Plynlimon to Chepstow.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Wye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buellt lay in the middle <b>Wye</b> and Irfon valleys around modern Builth Wells.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-buellt">Kingdom of Buellt</a></li><li><blockquote>After Roman rule ended a kingdom formed in the land between the Usk and the <b>Wye</b>, with traditions of a founder named Caradog Freichfras.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Kingdom of Gwent</a></li><li><blockquote>Gwrtheyrnion was absorbed by more powerful Welsh kingdoms, probably in the 10th century, and later formed a cantref of the Middle March between <b>Wye</b> and Severn, held by Welsh and Anglo-Norman lords such as the Mortimers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gwrtheyrnion">Kingdom of Gwrtheyrnion</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom covered the fertile vale and uplands between the Tywi and the <b>Wye</b>, with churches such as Llandaff and Llancarfan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-morgannwg">Kingdom of Morgannwg</a></li></ul></section>
